<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Masserano</h1><p class="st-meta">1598 CE – 1767 CE · capital Masserano</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny papal fief in northern Piedmont ruled by the Ferrero-Fieschi family until it was sold to the kings of Sardinia in 1767.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Masserano and Crevacuore, small towns in the hills between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biella">Biella</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesia">Sesia</a> valley, belonged to the Fieschi family and then to the Ferrero, who took the Fieschi name. In 1598 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii">Pope Clement VIII</a> raised them to a principality held as a fief of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vatican-city">Holy See</a>, giving the family sovereign status.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ferrero-Fieschi princes governed a few thousand subjects, minted coins and kept a small court, while often serving foreign rulers as soldiers and diplomats, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Surrounded by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy</a>, the state survived through papal protection and its rulers' connections abroad. Little else is recorded of its government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, now kings of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia</a>, long sought to absorb the enclave. In 1767 the prince ceded his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-iii-of-sardinia">Charles Emmanuel III</a>, ending the principality.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1598 CE – 1767 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.50%;width:3.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1598 CE – 1767 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Masserano</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Piedmontese, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Masserano and Crevacuore</dd></dl></aside></div>
